        "description": "<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/e77702f4-5825-4eff-aca4-480c42d661f8,1782577997907/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C7%2C1850%2C1040%29%3BResize%3D%28620%29' alt='A player wearing a blue shirt kicks a ball over the crossbar of a goal as a goalkeeper dives to the ground.' width='620' height='349' title='Italy&apos;s Roberto Baggio puts his penalty over the bar in the World Cup final against Brazil at the Rose Bowl in Pasadena, California July 17, 1994. Derided as a lottery by critics, the penalty shootout is unsurpassed as the ultimate test of nerve to decide tied games. The split-second moment can make a player a hero, or forever scar an otherwise unblemished career. Picture taken July 17, 1994.'/><p>With so much potentially at stake from a simple strike of a soccer ball, it's understandable that the science of penalty kicks has attracted an enormous amount of research. But when it comes to the intense pressure of a World Cup shootout, is the search for the perfect penalty",
